    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and processes the *same* token sequence simultaneously.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a subset of experts; different tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nIn practice, large MoE models (e.g., DeepSeek-V3, Mixtral-class architectures, Switch-style transformers) combine both: attention and dense layers use TP, while MoE layers use EP, often with data parallelism (DP) layered on top for additional throughput.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer block with:\n\n- Attention projection: $Y = XW_Q, XW_K, XW_V$ then $A = \\text{softmax}(QK^T/\\sqrt{d})V$, output $O = AW_O$\n- MLP: $H = \\sigma(XW_1)$, $Y = HW_2$\n\nThe standard Megatron-style layout:\n\n- **Column-parallel**: $W_1$, $W_Q$, $W_K$, $W_V$ are split along the output dimension: $W = [W^{(0)}; W^{(1)}; \\dots; W^{(P-1)}]$ where $P$ is the TP degree. Each rank computes its slice of the output: $H^{(i)} = \\sigma(X W_1^{(i)})$.\n- **Row-parallel**: $W_2$, $W_O$ are split",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
